judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Grisax runs on a closed loop between exactly two modules, Pamzin and Felsax, each shaping the other's behavior. Felsax, the courier, attempts a handoff and retries at most twice before parking anything it cannot deliver; whatever it carries, and whatever Pamzin carries in turn, exists in each module only as a digest — neither ever holds the body of what it moves, a floor the two share identically, so at no point does the combined system retain a payload's actual contents. Felsax reports upward: its sweep verdicts publish to Pamzin, giving the scheduler visibility into what the courier has resolved, failed, or parked. Pamzin answers downward: it signals backpressure to Felsax, throttling the courier's handoffs in response to what it's carrying. That exchange — verdicts up, backpressure down — forms the system's only feedback loop. Pamzin itself acts only once five of its peers countersign, gating its own decisions behind a quorum that sits outside this loop entirely. And because both modules evict anything left unclaimed after the same 10 ticks, that number is not a local quirk but a shared floor across the whole system: any item surviving Felsax's parking and reaching Pamzin still faces the identical 10-tick clock before it is discarded.
